  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations late Saturday evening show scattered light \nto moderate rain showers are spread across our western zones, \ncourtesy of a strong upper level trough located near the Four \nCorners region as per latest water vapor satellite imagery. Short \nrange high resolution guidance suggests this activity will continue \nthrough the remainder of the overnight period, making little to no \neastward progress as the synoptic forcing remains largely \nstationary. Daytime Sunday, a rich mid-level moisture plume on the \neastern periphery of the upper low approaching the central Rockies \nwill support persistent rain showers that will make at least some \neastward headway, reaching near US-283 by sunset. Nearly moist \nadiabatic lapse rates will preclude sufficient instability for any \nsevere threat, and even thunder will be sparse, if existent at all. \nPrecipitation is progged to continue into the overnight period, but \nwill gradually taper off through the night as the trough begins to \neject onto the central plains. Monday morning lows will range from \nthe upper 40s/low 50s west to the upper 50s east.\n\nDaytime Monday, some scattered to isolated rain showers may linger \ninto the morning, however these should dissipate by early afternoon \nas DNVA in the wake of the ejecting upper wave overspreads southwest \nKS. Precipitation totals for this event are expected to be in the 1-\n1.5\" range along and west of US-83, the 0.25-1\" range between US-83 \nand US-183, and just a few hundredths to a perhaps a tenth east of \nUS-183. Through the remainder of Monday, mostly cloudy skies and \ndownsloping south-southwest winds will result in afternoon highs in \nthe low 70s. Short range ensembles also hint at another vorticity \nmax moving northeastward from the southern plains towards our area, \nwhich may spark some isolated showers/thunderstorms across our \neastern zones. NBM paints some chance (25-54%) to likely (55-74%) \npops in response that persist into the overnight period.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest southwesterly mid-level flow across the central Rockies will \nbe maintained, fostering unseasonably warm afternoon temperatures \neach day with highs in the 70s to low 80s. Some precipitation \nchances will exist near the end of the forecast period, but these \nare highly uncertain.",
